EXT. BAMBOO FOREST - RAINY AFTERNOON

Tall green stalks creak in the wind. Mud everywhere. A trail disappears into fog.

HANNELORE BECK, 50s, a botanist, leads, machete in hand. Behind her trudges MARISOL AGUILAR, 20s, a student with a heavy pack. Last comes THABO NKOSI-WILLIAMS, 30s, their guide, who limps and carries a radio.

HANNELORE
(to all)
We stop at the next clearing.

MARISOL
(to Hannelore)
How far is that?

HANNELORE
Not far.

THABO
(to himself)
It is far.

Rain thickens. Marisol slips and grabs a stalk. It cracks.

MARISOL
Sorry!

THABO
Careful, it carries sound.

HANNELORE
Sound is fine. It's silence that worries me.

MARISOL
Why?

HANNELORE
Because it means something is listening.

They stop. All three listen. The rain pauses. A distant rustle.

MARISOL
Is that the wind?

THABO
(low)
No.

HANNELORE
Keep walking.

MARISOL
But which way?

THABO
Forward.

HANNELORE
Not forward. Left. Left!

MARISOL
Which left?

THABO
Mine.

Marisol veers right by mistake. Thabo yanks her back. She bumps into Hannelore. The three tangle for a second, then break apart.

MARISOL
Sorry, sorry.

HANNELORE
(to all)
Stay together.

THABO
(into radio, muttering)
This is Thabo. We are lost.

Static answers. He shakes the radio. It stays dead. He tucks it away.

THABO (CONT'D)
(to the group)
We keep moving.

HANNELORE
She knows.

THABO
She does?

MARISOL
I do?

HANNELORE
We keep moving.

They push on. The rustle follows. It never gets closer. It never gets farther.
